<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Our Routines – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Our Routines</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> First, I Sit Down.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: productive: Write simple words with model guidance. interactive: Engage in interactive labeling activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write simple words, with teacher support (e.g., 'pen' or 'door'). Can engage in interactive activities to label objects related to classroom routines (e.g., write 'book' under the picture of a book). Can copy simple words with guidance (e.g., 'sit' and 'go').</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification. Activating prior knowledge and introducing the writing focus for classroom objects and routine verbs.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a quick "Classroom Actions" TPR activity. "Let's sit down!" (Teacher points to chair, students sit). "Pick up your pens!" (Students mime picking up). "Put your bags on the desk!" (Students mime putting bags). This revisits key verbs and objects. The teacher then says, "Today, we will write these words!"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard with a picture of a 'pen' and the word 'pen' written clearly below it. The teacher says, "This is a pen." Then, the teacher writes the word 'pen' on the whiteboard, slowly and clearly, sounding out each letter: "/p/ /e/ /n/, pen." The teacher then writes the word 'sit', demonstrating letter formation and saying "/s/ /i/ /t/, sit." The teacher emphasizes initial sounds /p/ and /d/ by saying words like 'pick' and 'down' and asking students to repeat.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher points to the word 'pen' on the board and asks, "Is this a desk?" (Students shake heads 'no'). "Is this a pen?" (Students nod 'yes'). The teacher points to the letters and asks, "Can you see the letters?" (Students respond "Yes").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Our Routines". Presenting words for writing practice.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large flashcards (genre: illustrated words) displaying clear pictures of classroom objects and actions (e.g., 'chair', 'desk', 'bag', 'pen', 'paper', 'door', 'sit', 'pick up'). Each flashcard has the corresponding word written in a large, legible font. A simple chart depicting a two-step routine: "Step 1: Sit down." (picture of sitting) and "Step 2: Take paper." (picture of taking paper).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Students will recognize and match simple written words for classroom objects and actions in routines (A1.2, recognizing letter sounds A-E).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., 'chair'). Students identify the object orally: "Chair!" Then the teacher shows another flashcard (e.g., 'desk') and asks, "Is this a chair?" (Students say "No," "Desk!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher places several word flashcards (e.g., 'pen', 'door', 'desk') on the board. The teacher says, "Point to 'pen'." Students point to the correct word. The teacher repeats with other words, reinforcing the visual recognition of the written word.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing. Students practice forming letters and writing target words with guidance.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Writing simple words for classroom nouns (chair, desk, pen, paper, door, bag) and verbs (sit, pick up, go). Practicing letter formation and recognizing phonemic sounds /p/ and /d/ and letter sounds A-E. Grammar: Present simple ('This is a pen.'), imperatives ('Pick it up.'), sequencing words ('Step 1').</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Tracing and Copying (Controlled Practice):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with images of classroom objects (e.g., chair, desk, pen, paper, door) and simple action pictures (e.g., a child sitting, a child picking up). Below each image, there are dotted lines for tracing the corresponding word (e.g., "chair," "desk," "pen," "sit," "pick"). The teacher models tracing a word on the board, emphasizing starting points and direction. Students trace the dotted words. The teacher circulates, providing individual support for letter formation, reinforcing /p/ in 'pen' and /d' in 'desk'.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Picture Labeling with Word Bank (Semi-controlled):</b> On the same or a new worksheet, students see a set of new pictures of classroom objects (e.g., 'bag', 'notebook', 'eraser'). Beside these pictures, there is a small word bank with the correct words. Students choose the correct word from the bank and copy it onto the line below each picture to label it. The teacher prompts with sentences like, "This is a bag. Please write 'bag'."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Sequencing Routine Steps (Guided Copying):</b> The teacher displays a chart on the board with two or three simple routine steps using numbers and images, for example: "Step 1: Sit down." (picture of sitting). "Step 2: Pick up paper." (picture of picking up paper). Students receive a worksheet with these numbered steps and pictures, but with blanks for the key words. Students copy the words 'sit', 'down', 'pick', 'up', and 'paper' into the correct blanks, guided by the teacher's model on the board and verbal prompts (e.g., "First, I sit down. Please write 'sit'.").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-20 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Our Routines".</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Students will create a "My Classroom Routine" mini-poster by drawing simple images and labeling them with words they have practiced. This allows them to demonstrate their writing skills in the context of the theme "First, I Sit Down." and scenario "Our Routines."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher distributes a small piece of paper (or a pre-printed template) divided into 3 boxes.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In the first box, the teacher asks students to draw a picture of themselves sitting down at their desk. Below the drawing, there is a line where students write: "Sit down." (They can copy from the board or a word bank provided by the teacher).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In the second box, students draw a picture of a pen on a desk. Below the drawing, they write: "This is a pen."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">In the third box, students draw a picture of themselves picking up paper. Below, they write: "Pick up paper."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher circulates, offering support, prompting with polite expressions like, "Please write 'sit down'," and gently correcting letter formation or spelling. This encourages routine interactions and the use of imperatives.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment of students' writing ability and self-reflection.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the production task, noting student participation, effort in writing, and accuracy of copied words. Collect student mini-posters for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> The completed "My Classroom Routine" mini-poster with three labeled drawings.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Legible copying/writing of target words (e.g., 'sit', 'down', 'pen', 'pick', 'paper').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Correct matching of written word to the corresponding image.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates effort in letter formation and neatness.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Attempts to follow numbered sequencing when labeling routine steps.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are given a simple paper with two faces (happy/sad) and a thumbs up/down icon. They circle:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write words like 'pen' and 'sit'." (Thumbs up/down)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I like to write about our classroom." (Happy/sad face)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Review and planning for continuous improvement.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did you write today?" (Teacher asks students to show their posters and point to words).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What was fun about writing words?" (Students can respond with a word or gesture).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Did students successfully trace and copy simple words like 'pen', 'desk', 'sit', 'pick up'?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Were the instructions clear and adapted to the Kinder grade level for a writing focus?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What specific challenges did students face with letter formation or matching words to images? (e.g., initial sounds /p/ vs /d/ differentiation).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can I better scaffold writing for individual students or provide more fine motor skill practice for the next lesson?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> "Today we practiced writing words for our classroom routines and objects. Next time, in Lesson 5, we will use these words and our drawings to create a bigger routine chart for our classroom, putting all our ideas together!"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>